---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/deepDive.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

const title = "SNP Microarrays & Statistical Genotype Imputation: Hidden Markov Models and Population Reference Panels";
const description = "A mathematical and technological deep dive into genotyping microarrays, the tag SNP paradigm, Li & Stephens genealogical copying models, PBWT scaling, and HMM imputation across 30 million variants.";
/* Derived from this page's own content, not guessed: prose at 200 wpm, plus 15 s per
   display equation and 3 s per inline one. These constants had drifted to ~2.5x the
   truth (this page: 889 prose words, 6 display and 46 inline equations). Part 2 moves
   the series into a content collection where this is computed, not written down. */
const readingTime = "8 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article">
  <article class="deep-dive-article">
    <!-- Back to Catalog -->
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/deep_dives/gwas/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to GWAS Master Guide
      </a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="deep-dive-header">
      <div class="deep-dive-meta-bar" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-2);">
        <span class="deep-dive-tag">GWAS Foundations · Part 2</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-level-badge"><Icon name="book-open" size={14} /> Advanced & Mathematical</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="deep-dive-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="deep-dive-meta-bar">
        <span>By <strong>Kuan-Hao Chao</strong></span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>Published August 2026</span>
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Lead / Introduction -->
    <p class="deep-dive-lead">
      How can a genotyping array that measures only 500,000 physical markers provide complete, high-accuracy statistical data for over <strong>30 million genetic variants</strong> across hundreds of thousands of individuals? The answer lies at the intersection of biophysical surface chemistry, population linkage disequilibrium, and <strong>Hidden Markov Model (HMM) genealogical copying algorithms</strong>.
    </p>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="deep-dive-toc">
      <div class="deep-dive-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="deep-dive-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#microarrays">1. Physical Genotyping Microarrays: Mechanics & Chemistry</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tag-snps">2. The Tag SNP Paradigm & Linkage Redundancy</a></li>
        <li><a href="#li-stephens">3. The Li & Stephens Coalescent Copying Model</a></li>
        <li><a href="#hmm-math">4. Mathematical Formulation of the Imputation HMM</a></li>
        <li><a href="#pbwt">5. Algorithmic Scaling: Positional Burrows-Wheeler Transform (PBWT)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#reference-panels">6. Reference Panels: From HapMap to TOPMed</a></li>
        <li><a href="#dosages-info">7. Imputation Dosages & Quality Metrics (<Katex tex="R^2_{\text{info}}" />)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#summary">8. Summary & Next Steps in the GWAS Series</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="microarrays">
      <h2>1. Physical Genotyping Microarrays: Mechanics & Chemistry</h2>
      <p>
        High-throughput genotyping microarrays (such as the Illumina Infinium OmniExpress and Thermo Fisher Affymetrix Axiom arrays) revolutionized human genetics by lowering the cost per sample from thousands of dollars (for early sequencing) to under $30–$50 per individual.
      </p>

      <h3>The Illumina Infinium Assay Mechanics</h3>
      <p>
        1. <strong>Whole-Genome Amplification (WGA):</strong> Genomic DNA extracted from blood or saliva is isothermally amplified to generate microgram quantities.
        <br/>
        2. <strong>Enzymatic Fragmentation:</strong> DNA is cleaved into uniform 300–600 bp fragments.
        <br/>
        3. <strong>BeadChip Hybridization:</strong> Fragments anneal to 50-mer locus-specific oligonucleotide probes covalently anchored to microscopic silica beads in etched microwells.
        <br/>
        4. <strong>Single-Base Extension (SBE):</strong> DNA polymerase adds a single fluorescently labeled dideoxynucleotide (ddNTP) complementary to the polymorphic target base (e.g. Cy3 for A/T, Cy5 for C/G).
        <br/>
        5. <strong>Fluorescence Laser Scanning:</strong> High-resolution scanners measure dual-wavelength emission intensities:
        <br/>
        <Katex tex="R = I_{\text{Cy3}} + I_{\text{Cy5}}, \qquad \theta = \frac{2}{\pi} \arctan\left(\frac{I_{\text{Cy5}}}{I_{\text{Cy3}}}\right)" display={true} />
        A normalized intensity cluster plot maps each sample into discrete genotype calls: <Katex tex="\theta \approx 0 \implies AA" />, <Katex tex="\theta \approx 0.5 \implies AB" />, <Katex tex="\theta \approx 1.0 \implies BB" />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="tag-snps">
      <h2>2. The Tag SNP Paradigm & Linkage Redundancy</h2>
      <p>
        The human genome contains over 30 million common and low-frequency variants. Assaying every variant physically on a chip would be cost-prohibitive. However, because genetic markers located close to each other on a chromosome are inherited together in blocks of <strong>Linkage Disequilibrium (LD)</strong>, alleles at neighboring loci are highly correlated.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-math">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> The Tag SNP Principle
        </div>
        <p>
          A carefully selected subset of <strong>500,000 to 2,000,000 "Tag SNPs"</strong> can act as statistical proxies for the remaining 28+ million variants, capturing over 85–95% of genome-wide common variation at <Katex tex="r^2 \ge 0.8" />.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="li-stephens">
      <h2>3. The Li & Stephens Coalescent Copying Model</h2>
      <p>
        In 2003, Na Li and Matthew Stephens published a foundational genealogical model for Linkage Disequilibrium. Instead of simulating the full ancestral recombination graph (ARG), they approximated the probability distribution of a new haplotype as an <strong>imperfect mosaic copy of known reference haplotypes</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-biology">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> Intuitive Mosaic Copying
        </div>
        <p>
          Every human chromosome is a biological mosaic formed by historical meiotic crossovers among ancestral chromosomes. If we have a dense reference panel of fully sequenced haplotypes (e.g. 1000 Genomes or TOPMed), any individual's array data can be modeled as jumping between ancestral reference templates, with occasional mismatching mutations.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="hmm-math">
      <h2>4. Mathematical Formulation of the Imputation HMM</h2>
      <p>
        Let <Katex tex="\mathbf{H} = \{h_1, h_2, \dots, h_K\}" /> be a reference panel of <Katex tex="K" /> fully sequenced haplotypes across <Katex tex="M" /> marker loci, where <Katex tex="h_{k, m} \in \{0, 1\}" />.
        <br/>
        Let <Katex tex="\mathbf{G} = \{g_1, g_2, \dots, g_L\}" /> be the target sample's observed genotypes at <Katex tex="L \ll M" /> array markers.
      </p>

      <h3>Hidden States</h3>
      <p>
        At each marker locus <Katex tex="m" />, the hidden state <Katex tex="S_m = k \in \{1, \dots, K\}" /> indicates which reference haplotype template is currently being copied.
      </p>

      <h3>Transition Probabilities (Recombination Jumps)</h3>
      <p>
        The probability of transitioning (crossover) between template <Katex tex="k" /> at locus <Katex tex="m-1" /> and template <Katex tex="k'" /> at locus <Katex tex="m" /> separated by physical distance <Katex tex="d_m" /> (genetic map distance <Katex tex="c_m" /> in Morgans) is:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">HMM Transition Probability Matrix</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="P(S_m = k' \mid S_{m-1} = k) = \begin{cases} (1 - \rho_m) + \frac{\rho_m}{K}, & \text{if } k' = k \\ \frac{\rho_m}{K}, & \text{if } k' \neq k \end{cases}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\rho_m = 1 - \exp\left( -\frac{4 N_e c_m}{K} \right)" /> represents the locus-specific recombination crossover probability, where <Katex tex="N_e" /> is the effective population size.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Emission Probabilities (Genotyping Errors & Mutations)</h3>
      <p>
        The probability of observing allele <Katex tex="g_m" /> given that the hidden copied template allele is <Katex tex="h_{k, m}" />:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">HMM Emission Probability</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="P(g_m \mid S_m = k) = \begin{cases} 1 - \epsilon_m, & \text{if } g_m = h_{k, m} \\ \epsilon_m, & \text{if } g_m \neq h_{k, m} \end{cases}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\epsilon_m = \frac{\theta}{2(K + \theta)}" /> models the mutation and genotyping error rate under Watterson's coalescent estimator.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Forward-Backward Posterior Decoding</h3>
      <p>
        Using the <strong>Forward-Backward algorithm</strong>, we compute the posterior probability <Katex tex="\gamma_m(k) = P(S_m = k \mid \mathbf{G})" /> for every reference haplotype. The imputed probability of an unmeasured allele <Katex tex="x \in \{0, 1\}" /> at locus <Katex tex="m" /> is:
        <br/>
        <Katex tex="P(Y_m = 1 \mid \mathbf{G}) = \sum_{k=1}^K \gamma_m(k) \cdot h_{k, m}" display={true} />
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="pbwt">
      <h2>5. Algorithmic Scaling: Positional Burrows-Wheeler Transform (PBWT)</h2>
      <p>
        Standard HMM forward-backward computation requires <Katex tex="\mathcal{O}(M \cdot K^2)" /> operations per individual. When reference panels expanded from 1,000 haplotypes (HapMap) to >200,000 haplotypes (TOPMed), exact HMM calculation became computationally intractable.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-math">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="cpu" size={15} /> Richard Durbin's PBWT Breakthrough (2014)
        </div>
        <p>
          The <strong>Positional Burrows-Wheeler Transform (PBWT)</strong> sorts reference haplotypes at each site by their reversed prefix history. Because identical shared sequence blocks cluster adjacently in the sorted PBWT order, HMM template search runs in <strong><Katex tex="\mathcal{O}(M \cdot K)" /> or sub-linear time</strong>, enabling tools like <strong>IMPUTE5</strong>, <strong>Beagle 5</strong>, and <strong>Minimac4</strong> to impute millions of genomes in minutes!
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 6 -->
    <section id="reference-panels">
      <h2>6. Reference Panels: From HapMap to TOPMed</h2>

      <div class="summary-table-wrap">
        <table class="summary-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Reference Panel</th>
              <th>Sample Size (<Katex tex="K" /> Haplotypes)</th>
              <th>Total Imputable Variants</th>
              <th>Key Diversity Features</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>HapMap Phase II / III</strong></td>
              <td>~270 to 1,301 samples</td>
              <td>~2.5 to 4 Million SNPs</td>
              <td>First multi-population catalog (CEU, YRI, CHB, JPT).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>1000 Genomes Project (1kG Phase 3)</strong></td>
              <td>2,504 samples (5,008 haplotypes)</td>
              <td>~88 Million variants (SNPs + Indels)</td>
              <td>26 global populations; gold-standard public multi-ancestry panel.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Haplotype Reference Consortium (HRC)</strong></td>
              <td>32,488 samples (64,976 haplotypes)</td>
              <td>~40 Million SNPs</td>
              <td>Massive European cohort; high accuracy for low-frequency variants (<Katex tex="\text{MAF} \ge 0.1\%" />).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>TOPMed (Trans-Omics for Precision Medicine)</strong></td>
              <td>>100,000 deep WGS samples (>200,000 haplotypes)</td>
              <td>>300 Million variants</td>
              <td>Deep 30x whole-genome sequencing across diverse multi-ethnic cohorts.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 7 -->
    <section id="dosages-info">
      <h2>7. Imputation Dosages & Quality Metrics (<Katex tex="R^2_{\text{info}}" />)</h2>

      <h3>Continuous Genotype Dosages</h3>
      <p>
        Imputation does not output hard discrete integer calls (<Katex tex="0, 1, 2" />). Instead, for an individual <Katex tex="i" /> at variant <Katex tex="j" />, it calculates posterior probabilities for the three possible genotypes: <Katex tex="p_0 = P(G_{ij}=0)" />, <Katex tex="p_1 = P(G_{ij}=1)" />, <Katex tex="p_2 = P(G_{ij}=2)" /> where <Katex tex="p_0 + p_1 + p_2 = 1" />.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Expected Genotype Dosage</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="G_{ij} = \mathbb{E}[G_{ij}] = 0 \cdot p_0 + 1 \cdot p_1 + 2 \cdot p_2 = p_1 + 2 p_2 \in [0.0, 2.0]" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>The Imputation Quality Score (<Katex tex="R^2_{\text{info}}" /> / Mach <Katex tex="r^2" />)</h3>
      <p>
        How confident is the HMM in its predictions? If the panel has insufficient LD coverage, posterior probabilities will collapse to population allele frequency averages (<Katex tex="p_0 = (1-p)^2, p_1 = 2p(1-p), p_2 = p^2" />), and dosage variance will drop to 0.
        <br/>
        The <strong>INFO score (<Katex tex="R^2_{\text{info}}" />)</strong> compares the observed empirical variance of the imputed dosages against the expected binomial variance under Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Imputation Quality Metric Formulation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="R^2_{\text{info}} = 1 - \frac{\sum_{i=1}^N \left( p_{i1} + 4p_{i2} - G_{ij}^2 \right)}{2 N \hat{p}_j (1 - \hat{p}_j)} = \frac{\text{Var}(G_j)}{2 \hat{p}_j (1 - \hat{p}_j)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="R^2_{\text{info}} = 1.0" /> &rarr; Perfect certainty (matches physical genotyping).
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="R^2_{\text{info}} \ge 0.8" /> &rarr; Standard high-confidence threshold for GWAS association testing.
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="R^2_{\text{info}} < 0.3 - 0.5" /> &rarr; Poor imputation quality; variant must be discarded.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 8 -->
    <section id="summary">
      <h2>8. Summary & Next Steps in the GWAS Series</h2>
      <p>
        Statistical genotype imputation transforms modest genotyping microarrays into dense, 30-million-variant datasets by leveraging the ancestral recombination structure encoded in large reference panels.
      </p>

      <div class="algo-promo-card">
        <h3>Continue to Part 3: Rigorous Quality Control (QC) Protocols</h3>
        <p>
          Discover the critical sample- and variant-level filtering protocols required to eliminate batch effects, sample swaps, and false-positive skyscrapers.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/gwas-quality-control/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Read Part 3: Quality Control Protocols</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
